Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Scan Sector 4 Never Clears
- Story & Context: During the first scan set, Mina discovers that one radar display continues showing a faint arc even though the system has no power. The image is only phosphor persistence from an old test source, but the band builds the track around its slow fade. Akiho sustains one sho cluster for nearly a minute while Tariq places only three kick events underneath. Ren answers with a descending baritone line drenched in spring reverb, then hard-mutes the tail each time the arc dims. The final section removes the drums entirely; the track ends when the display finally goes dark.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old circular radar display in a dark coastal operations room, faint green arc fading across black glass, sho pipes reflected in the screen, coiled cable, salt-corroded metal, one white work light. Spoken fragments limited to “sector four,” “still visible,” and “do not refresh.”

Track 2: Salt in the Relay Cabinet
- Story & Context: After a storm, Tariq opens a relay cabinet and finds salt residue along the lower contacts where wind-driven spray has entered through the building louvers. Rather than cleaning it before rehearsal, Mina contact-mics the cabinet and records the brittle crackle produced when the metal door flexes. Ren turns the rhythm of those cracks into muted baritone-guitar bridge strikes while Akiho shifts between two closely related sho clusters without changing the drone center. The chorus is defined by subtraction: every time the guitar reverb becomes widest, the grime drums vanish for one full bar.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro documentary photograph of white salt crystals on oxidized relay contacts, black contact microphone clipped to the cabinet wall, baritone-guitar bridge blurred in the foreground, sho case on the floor, cold gray-blue coastal light. Vocal material limited to practical phrases: “leave the residue,” “record before cleaning,” and “close the door slowly.”

Track 3: Antenna Stops Facing North
- Story & Context: During a maintenance test, the disabled antenna platform is manually rotated a few degrees and fails to return to its marked north position. The mechanical error becomes the basis of the track. Tariq samples the ratcheting movement and creates a 140 BPM pulse from its uneven clicks. Ren tunes the tremolo rate to the ratchet cycle, while Akiho holds a sho cluster whose lowest note remains constant as the upper voices change. The band deliberately refuses to correct the antenna alignment before the recording ends. In the final minute, the sub-bass disappears and only sho, motor clicks, and guitar harmonics remain, leaving the track harmonically suspended and physically misaligned.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Decommissioned radar antenna platform on a coastal roof at night, painted north alignment mark visibly offset from the actual antenna position, wet metal, low clouds, sho player seen through an interior window, dark baritone guitar leaning against a cabinet, restrained industrial realism. Spoken fragments: “three degrees west,” “leave it there,” and “north is only the mark.”

